<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taishō period</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taisho</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taishō period was the era of Japanese history corresponding to the reign of Emperor Taishō, from 1912 to 1926. It is associated with a growth of party politics, parliamentary government and liberal ideas, often called Taishō democracy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genro">genro</a>, an informal council of elder statesmen, provided coherent governance through the Meiji and Taishō periods, but left no institutional successor as military factions gained power in the 1930s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taisho-period.jpg" alt="Taishō period" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imperial Household Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Taisho_of_Japan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
